Part 5 — fighter-colored Void explosion

When a fighter crosses the stage Void boundary and gets removed from play, create a short visual explosion exactly at the location where the fighter is swallowed.

This is a presentation effect only.

It must not:

* change velocity
* deal damage
* affect Launch Point
* alter scoring
* affect respawn timing
* affect hitstop
* modify collision
* alter the Void boundary
* delay lostToVoid
* change match results
* change CPU behavior

Trigger point

The clean shared trigger is Arena.checkVoid().

For every fighter in that step’s taken list:

1. capture the fighter’s disappearance position and palette;
2. create the visual burst;
3. mark/process the fighter as lost exactly as the existing code does.

Do this once per Void loss.

It must work when:

* one fighter falls;
* both fighters fall on the same fixed step;
* a fighter falls in Practice;
* a fighter falls in Quick Battle;
* a fighter falls in Watch Mode;
* the fall wins the match and the fighter never respawns.

Do not tie the effect to Battle.onVoid() because Practice also needs it.

Explosion position

Snapshot a world-space position before the fighter is removed.

Center it on the fighter rather than blindly using the feet.

A reasonable baseline is around:

* x: body center
* y: approximately body vertical center

Use the actual body/render representation already available in Fighter rather than hard-coded stage coordinates.

The explosion must stay at that recorded world-space point even after the fighter has been removed from inPlay.

Palette

The effect must visually match whichever fighter disappeared.

Do not give every fighter the existing amber hit-spark color.

Do not use Alva’s interface green unless that green genuinely belongs to the fighter art.

Implement this in a data-driven way.

Preferred approach:

Add character presentation metadata such as a small Void-FX palette to the character definition, for example 3–5 colors derived from that fighter’s actual idle/walk sprites.

Do not put code such as:

if (fighter.id === '0001') ... else if (fighter.id === '0002') ...

inside the effect renderer.

Instead the FX system should simply consume fighter data.

Inspect the real sprite colors and choose representative shades for both currently available fighters.

Include:

* a dominant/base color;
* a brighter readable color;
* one or more supporting shades.

Because the Void itself is very dark, ensure a fighter whose palette contains a lot of dark pixels still produces a visible burst. If necessary, derive a lighter tint from one of that fighter’s own colors rather than introducing an unrelated hue.

A generic fallback is fine for future characters whose definition temporarily lacks palette metadata, but both #0001 and #0002 must have proper palette-matched results.

Visual design

Keep it consistent with Alva’s crisp 2D presentation.

The effect should feel like the fighter was abruptly consumed/disintegrated by the Void:

* quick central flash/pop;
* expanding burst/ring;
* several short outward fragments, rays, or pixel-like particles;
* fragments using multiple shades from the fighter’s own palette;
* fast fade;
* approximately 0.3–0.5 seconds total;
* no lingering screen clutter.

Favor crisp/blocky/pixel-art-compatible geometry over soft realistic smoke.

No gore.

No giant full-screen explosion.

The effect should be noticeable without obscuring the match.

Use deterministic/semi-deterministic seeded variation like the existing hit sparks where appropriate so tests and repeated rendering remain stable.

Effect lifetime

Extend HitEffects cleanly with something like a collection of active Void bursts.

For example, conceptually:

* voidBursts = []
* takeVoid(...)
* age/remove them from update(dt)
* drawVoidBursts(...)

Names can differ if a cleaner design fits the codebase.

Each burst needs enough snapshotted data to survive after the fighter object leaves play:

* x/y
* age
* lifetime
* palette
* seed/shape information as needed

Do not retain unnecessary references to dead fighter state.

Clear the effects appropriately when the arena is destroyed.

Render order

This is important.

Arena.render() currently draws:

* gameplay objects/effects;
* foreground;
* the Void itself;
* then screen-space fighter status/debug UI.

If the explosion is drawn before the Void layer, the black Void may cover the exact effect we are trying to show.

Render the Void explosion after theme.drawVoid() so it is visible at the swallowing point.

Keep markers/status rules unchanged: a fighter that is out of play must still have no marker, Energy bar, shadow, or sprite.

Reduced motion

Respect the existing reduced-motion philosophy.

The Void burst itself can still appear because it communicates a game event, but do not add new camera shake, zoom, or large movement solely for this effect when reduced motion is enabled.

The burst can use a simplified/less expansive animation under reduced motion if needed.

Do not alter the existing lethal-launch presentation beyond integrating cleanly with it.

